1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,523
Look down into the abyss.

2
00:00:02,473 --> 00:00:08,789
Thirty-three meters below the mountain ridge, three colossal trenches have severed the living rock.

3
00:00:08,789 --> 00:00:18,893
Standing in the center of the chasm is an isolated island of volcanic basalt: fifty meters long, thirty-three meters wide, and one hundred feet tall.

4
00:00:18,893 --> 00:00:23,190
Four hundred thousand tons of rock have vanished into the valley.

5
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:27,343
Now, silence falls over the Charanandri ridge.

6
00:00:27,343 --> 00:00:30,963
Ten thousand quarrymen rest their sledgehammers.

7
00:00:30,963 --> 00:00:34,270
For months, they have destroyed the mountain.

8
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:37,486
Now, they must perform a miracle.

9
00:00:37,486 --> 00:00:45,130
Master architect Kokasa stands at the sheer edge of the trench, staring across the void at the naked stone island.

10
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:48,971
The rock is raw, jagged, and unformed.

11
00:00:48,971 --> 00:00:53,984
Inside that monolithic block sleeps the greatest temple ever conceived.

12
00:00:54,148 --> 00:00:58,954
In the royal camp of King Krishna the First, crisis loomed.

13
00:00:58,904 --> 00:01:04,542
According to the medieval chronicle Katha-Kalpataru, the queen had fallen gravely ill.

14
00:01:04,542 --> 00:01:14,021
In her fever, she made a terrifying vow: she would touch neither food nor water until she beheld the gleaming pinnacle of a temple dedicated to Shiva.

15
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:16,873
The royal architects were summoned.

16
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:19,620
They bowed their heads in despair.

17
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:26,938
To quarry the foundations, raise the walls, and mount the spire of a traditional temple would take twenty years.

18
00:01:26,938 --> 00:01:31,704
Before the first pillar was raised, the queen would starve to death.

19
00:01:31,704 --> 00:01:36,014
Then, master builder Kokasa stepped before the throne.

20
00:01:36,014 --> 00:01:43,735
He made an impossible promise: Within seven days, my Queen shall gaze upon the crowning finial of Shiva's abode.

21
00:01:43,735 --> 00:01:46,469
The court whispered in disbelief.

22
00:01:46,469 --> 00:01:50,766
How could any man build a thirty-meter temple in a single week?

23
00:01:50,932 --> 00:01:57,939
Kokasa knew a secret that every modern engineer respects: You do not build a rock-cut temple from the bottom up.

24
00:01:57,889 --> 00:02:00,428
You carve it from the top down.

25
00:02:00,428 --> 00:02:07,368
Traditional construction is additive: you assemble stone on stone, correcting mistakes as you climb.

26
00:02:07,368 --> 00:02:11,183
Rock-cut architecture is ruthlessly subtractive.

27
00:02:11,183 --> 00:02:14,178
Every stroke of the chisel is permanent.

28
00:02:14,178 --> 00:02:22,173
There is no scaffolding resting on the courtyard, because the courtyard floor is still trapped under one hundred feet of solid bedrock.

29
00:02:22,173 --> 00:02:27,342
If an artisan cuts two inches too deep into a lintel, the roof cracks.

30
00:02:27,342 --> 00:02:33,371
If a fissure is miscalculated, a hundred tons of overhead stone collapses into dust.

31
00:02:33,371 --> 00:02:36,365
You cannot glue a mountain back together.

32
00:02:36,365 --> 00:02:40,662
To save the queen, Kokasa would not dig into the earth.

33
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:43,032
He would climb to the sky.

34
00:02:43,204 --> 00:02:50,562
At dawn, the master surveyors, the Sutradharas, climbed ropes to the summit of the lonely stone island.

35
00:02:50,512 --> 00:02:53,325
They carried no iron chisels.

36
00:02:53,325 --> 00:02:58,090
They brought the Shanku: a calibrated wooden solar shadow pole.

37
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:05,122
As the morning sun crested the Sahyadri ranges, the shadow of the gnomon tracked across the virgin basalt.

38
00:03:05,122 --> 00:03:12,765
By bisecting the shadow's morning and evening arcs, they established the true cardinal east-west axis of the universe.

39
00:03:12,765 --> 00:03:16,163
Next came the sacred cord, the Sutra.

40
00:03:16,163 --> 00:03:24,197
Dipped in wet vermilion red chalk, the chords were stretched taut across the stone and snapped against the basalt with sharp twangs.

41
00:03:24,197 --> 00:03:32,361
Grid lines bloomed across the peak: the Vastu-Purusha Mandala, a mathematical matrix of sixty-four sacred squares.

42
00:03:32,361 --> 00:03:40,109
Every sanctuary, doorway, and column was calculated down to the millimeter before a single chip of stone was struck.

43
00:03:40,276 --> 00:03:46,293
On the second morning, the Rupakaras—the master sculptors—ascended the summit.

44
00:03:46,243 --> 00:03:52,037
At the exact center of the mandala, Kokasa placed the point of a tempered steel chisel.

45
00:03:52,037 --> 00:03:54,043
A hammer struck.

46
00:03:54,043 --> 00:03:58,353
The sound echoed down into the empty trenches like a gunshot.

47
00:03:58,353 --> 00:04:00,853
The sacred work had begun.

48
00:04:00,853 --> 00:04:06,868
They did not cut downward into the rock; they isolated the crowning finial, the Stupi.

49
00:04:06,868 --> 00:04:18,522
Working in a tight circle on their knees, master artisans chipped away the surrounding basalt, releasing a massive, three-tiered ribbed stone vase, the Kalasha, from the living mountain.

50
00:04:18,522 --> 00:04:25,944
Unlike built temples where the finial is hoisted by cranes, this four-ton sacred crown was never lifted an inch.

51
00:04:25,944 --> 00:04:31,699
It was born directly from the volcanic peak, permanently anchored to the bedrock of Earth.

52
00:04:31,876 --> 00:04:39,026
With the finial carved, sculptors stepped downward onto narrow rock ledges, cutting the spire beneath their feet.

53
00:04:38,976 --> 00:04:43,664
This was the Vimana: the thirty-meter Dravidian pyramidal tower.

54
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:48,559
Tier by tier, the stone was peeled away like the layers of an onion.

55
00:04:48,559 --> 00:04:56,463
On the highest level, they sculpted the octagonal stone neck, the Griva, flanked by four resting bulls of Shiva.

56
00:04:56,463 --> 00:05:06,541
Below it, they hewed the stepped horizontal storeys, carving miniature pavilion shrines—the Kutas and Salas—directly into the descending slopes.

57
00:05:06,541 --> 00:05:14,419
Under the blazing sun, hundreds of sculptors worked shoulder to shoulder, suspended by hemp ropes over thirty-meter drops.

58
00:05:14,419 --> 00:05:18,195
The chisel dust rose like incense into the sky.

59
00:05:18,195 --> 00:05:26,320
By the fifth day, the stepped pyramid of the spire emerged from the rock, perfectly proportioned, tapering toward the heavens.

60
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:33,025
Now came the most dangerous moment of the entire excavation: Hollowing the mountain from the inside out.

61
00:05:32,975 --> 00:05:38,379
Beneath the sculpted spire lay the Garbhagriha—the inner sanctum of Shiva.

62
00:05:38,379 --> 00:05:45,319
To create this sacred cave, stonemasons had to bore horizontally into the rock beneath the finished roof.

63
00:05:45,319 --> 00:05:52,077
They worked in suffocating darkness, illuminated only by bronze oil lamps burning sesame oil.

64
00:05:52,077 --> 00:05:58,652
Standing on temporary timber planks, masons chipped away the stone ceiling above their own heads.

65
00:05:58,652 --> 00:06:05,801
One wrong blow could fracture the basalt roof slab, bringing the multi-ton spire crashing down upon them.

66
00:06:05,801 --> 00:06:13,431
They tested the thickness of the stone by striking the ceiling with wooden mallets, listening to the acoustic resonance through the rock.

67
00:06:13,431 --> 00:06:18,639
A deep, hollow ring meant stop; a solid thud meant cut further.

68
00:06:18,639 --> 00:06:24,095
In twenty-four hours of nonstop carving, the sanctum ceiling was perfected.

69
00:06:24,268 --> 00:06:28,593
On the dawn of the seventh day, the miracle was unveiled.

70
00:06:28,543 --> 00:06:38,959
The artisans coated the newly carved shikhara with a fine white plaster of calcined seashell lime, burnishing it with smooth agates until it gleamed like Himalayan snow.

71
00:06:38,959 --> 00:06:45,561
From the royal pavilion miles away across the valley, the Queen looked toward the Charanandri ridge.

72
00:06:45,561 --> 00:06:54,532
There, rising above the dark basalt cliff, gleamed the white pinnacle of Kailasa, shining like the crown of Shiva in the morning sun.

73
00:06:54,532 --> 00:06:57,110
The Queen broke her fast.

74
00:06:57,110 --> 00:06:59,727
Kokasa had kept his vow.

75
00:06:59,727 --> 00:07:05,886
Yet beneath that radiant spire, eighty feet of dark, solid mountain remained untouched.

76
00:07:05,886 --> 00:07:10,300
Kokasa looked down from the summit into the shadowed abyss below.

77
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,566
The crown was carved.

78
00:07:12,566 --> 00:07:22,839
Now, they had to excavate the body: slicing through thirty meters of stone to carve suspended bridges, monolithic elephants, and the great hypostyle hall.

79
00:07:22,839 --> 00:07:27,970
Next week, on Episode Four: The Floating Bridges of Kailasa.

80
00:07:27,970 --> 00:07:31,381
Subscribe, and carve the impossible.

